# Normalize line endings for every text file in the repository.
#
# Without this, the repo's line endings depend on each contributor's
# `core.autocrlf` setting. This checkout happens to have `core.autocrlf=input`,
# so it produced LF blobs — but that is a local setting, not a property of the
# repository, and a contributor with the Windows default (`autocrlf=true`) would
# commit CRLF into files that must not have it.
#
# It matters concretely here for two reasons:
#
#   1. The `benchmarks/*.json` artifacts are committed evidence and are diffed
#      across runs and machines. A CRLF flip would show every line as changed and
#      bury the numbers that actually moved. The `bench --out` flag writes LF
#      explicitly for the same reason.
#   2. `cargo fmt --check` and the on-disk format tests are byte-sensitive; CRLF
#      creeping into a source file is an avoidable CI failure.
#
# `text=auto` would let git guess per file. `text eol=lf` is deliberate instead:
# every text file in this repo is LF, on every platform, with no guessing.
* text eol=lf

# Explicit binary marking, so git never attempts EOL conversion or a textual
# diff on these. None are committed today; these entries exist so that adding a
# corrupted-segment fixture or an image cannot silently be mangled on checkout,
# which is exactly the failure mode this file is here to prevent.
*.wal   binary
*.bin   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.pdf   binary

# Rust/Cargo generated lockfile is text and must stay LF; called out explicitly
# because it is machine-written and would otherwise be easy to overlook.
Cargo.lock text eol=lf
